course: euphemisms
lesson: 19
title: Soft words for hard things: money and poverty
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Being poor can feel embarrassing, so people often talk about it with soft, roundabout words.
explain: Old-fashioned speakers say "in reduced circumstances"; friends say "hard up" or "a bit short"; officials say "food insecure" or "receiving public assistance".
explain: Some words are kind, like "less fortunate"; some are polite cover, like "careful with money" for someone stingy.
explain: For example, a man who says he is "temporarily embarrassed" at the till is joking that he has run out of money.
fact: "Fallen on hard times" means someone has become poor after being better off.
fact: "Of modest means" means poor or not rich.
fact: "In reduced circumstances" (old-fashioned UK) means poorer than before.
fact: "In straitened circumstances" (old-fashioned) means poor and short of money.
fact: "Hard up" (everyday, especially UK) means short of money.
fact: "Cash-strapped" means short of money, often said of councils, schools or families.
fact: "Temporarily embarrassed" is an old-fashioned joke meaning out of money for now.
fact: "A little short" means without enough money to pay.
fact: "Person experiencing homelessness" (official US) means a homeless person.
fact: "Rough sleeper" (official UK) means a homeless person who sleeps outside.
fact: "Food insecure" (official) means hungry and not able to afford enough food.
fact: "Receiving public assistance" (official US) means living on welfare: money the government gives to people who need help.
fact: "Budget-conscious" means poor or unwilling to spend.
fact: "Careful with money" is a kind way to say stingy (hating to spend or share money).
fact: "Less fortunate" means poor.
fact: "Disadvantaged background" means someone grew up poor.
fact: "Deprived area" (official UK) means a poor neighbourhood.
fact: "Inner city" (especially US) is often used to mean a poor urban neighbourhood.
fact: "The wrong side of the tracks" (US) means the poor part of town.
fact: "On a fixed income" (US) means having little money, usually as a pensioner, an old person living on a pension (money paid after you stop work).
exercise: "The family has fallen on hard times." What happened? | They became poor after being better off | They fell down some stairs | They had bad weather | They are always late
exercise: What does "a woman of modest means" mean? | She is not rich | She is very shy | She does not boast | She is small and quiet
exercise: An old novel says a lady lives "in reduced circumstances". What does it mean? | She is poorer than before | She lives in a smaller town | She has lost weight | She has fewer rules
exercise: What does "in straitened circumstances" mean? | Poor and short of money | Living on a straight road | Very well organised | Stuck in a narrow place
exercise: A British student says he is "hard up". What does he mean? | He is short of money | He is very tired | He is in trouble at school | He is standing up straight
exercise: The news says the council is "cash-strapped". What does it mean? | It is short of money | It keeps money in a strong box | It has just been paid | It only takes cash
exercise: A man at the till says he is "temporarily embarrassed". What does he mean? | He has run out of money for now | He blushed for a moment | He forgot his glasses | He tripped over
exercise: "I'm a little short this week, can I pay you Friday?" What is the problem? | Not enough money to pay | He is not very tall | The week is too short | He is in a hurry
exercise: A US charity talks about "persons experiencing homelessness". Who are they? | Homeless people | People moving house | People on holiday | People building homes
exercise: A UK council counts "rough sleepers". Who are they? | Homeless people sleeping outside | People who snore loudly | Campers in a field | People with no beds at home
exercise: A report says many families are "food insecure". What does it mean? | They cannot afford enough food | They keep their food locked up | They are afraid of new food | Their food is unsafe to eat
exercise: What does "receiving public assistance" mean in the US? | Living on government welfare | Getting help crossing the road | Being helped by a librarian | Getting a prize in public
exercise: A shop advert is for "budget-conscious shoppers". Who are they? | People who are poor or do not want to spend | People who count their steps | People who love expensive things | People who work in banks
exercise: Aunt says Uncle Bob is "careful with money". What is she really saying? | He is stingy | He never drops coins | He is a banker | He is very generous
exercise: "We collect toys for the less fortunate." Who are they? | Poor people | Unlucky gamblers | People who lost a game | People with no friends
exercise: A footballer came from a "disadvantaged background". What does it mean? | He grew up poor | He grew up behind the stadium | He was bad at football | He came from far away
exercise: The government gives money to "deprived areas". What are they? | Poor neighbourhoods | Areas with no trees | Places with no shops at night | Places without sleep
exercise: In US news, "inner city schools" often means schools where? | In poor city neighbourhoods | In the exact middle of a city | Inside a big building | In rich downtown towers
exercise: "He grew up on the wrong side of the tracks." What does it mean? | He grew up in the poor part of town | He lived next to a railway | He took the wrong train | He was lost as a child
exercise: Grandma lives "on a fixed income". What does it mean? | She has little money, usually a pension | Her money never runs out | She fixes things for money | She earns more every year
test: "Can't come to the cinema, I'm proper hard up mate" — why can't he come? | He has no money | He is ill | He is too tall for the seats | He is grounded
test: A news line: "Cash-strapped council closes three libraries." Why are the libraries closing? | The council is short of money | The libraries are too old | Nobody reads any more | The council has too much money
test: Gossip: "Old Mrs Pike's in reduced circumstances since her husband went, poor dear." What happened to Mrs Pike? | She is poorer than before | She moved to a small flat by choice | She has become very ill | She won the lottery
test: "Uncle Dave is so careful with money he reuses tea bags lol" — what is Uncle Dave? | Stingy | Very rich | Clumsy | Generous
test: A politician says: "We will support residents of our most deprived areas." Who will be helped? | People in the poorest neighbourhoods | People who lost their homes in a storm | People who need more sleep | People who live in the countryside
test: Tom reckons "food insecure" means scared of vegetables. Right? | No, it means not able to afford enough food | Yes, it means a picky eater | Yes, it means allergic | No, it means the fridge is broken
test: A man at the market: "Ah, I find myself temporarily embarrassed — could you hold these apples till tomorrow?" Why does he ask? | He has no money with him | He is ashamed of the apples | He is going on holiday | He forgot his bag
test: "Your nan's on a fixed income, so don't expect a big present, ok?" Why no big present? | Nan has little money | Nan does not like presents | Nan is busy fixing things | Nan already bought one
test: A charity sign: "Your coins help rough sleepers stay warm tonight." Who is helped? | Homeless people sleeping outside | Children having a sleepover | People who sleep badly | Campers in the hills
test: "she's from the wrong side of the tracks, my mum says, as if that matters" — what does the mum mean? | The girl comes from the poor part of town | The girl lives by a railway | The girl missed her train | The girl is good at running
test: An advert: "Holidays for the budget-conscious traveller!" Who is the advert for? | People with little money to spend | People who plan every minute | People who love luxury | People who travel for work
test: A famous singer says, "I came from a disadvantaged background." What does he mean? | He grew up poor | He grew up abroad | He was a bad singer at first | He sat at the back of class
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a household described as "in straitened circumstances"? | The household is poor and short of money | The household is situated on a straight road | The household is exceptionally well organised | The household is confined to a narrow space
exam: It is given that a municipal authority is described as "cash-strapped". Identify the principal meaning of this description. | The authority has insufficient funds | The authority retains its funds in a secured box | The authority has recently received payment | The authority accepts payment in cash only
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the official expression "person experiencing homelessness". | A person without a home | A person in the process of relocating | A person engaged in the construction of dwellings | A person on holiday away from home
exam: Whereas an individual is stated to be "receiving public assistance" in the United States, determine the circumstance thereby indicated. | The individual is in receipt of government welfare | The individual is being aided in a public place | The individual has received a public award | The individual is employed in public service
